issues.problematic_code_snippet:
/* Validate that stored-key to key conversion works correctly */
static int validateStoredKeyConversion(dict *d, const void *key __stored_key) {
    const void *extracted = dictStoredKey2Key(d, key);
    if (d->type->keyFromStoredKey) {
        return extracted != NULL;
    }
    return extracted == key;
}

/* Before the key is released, obtain the link to
 * ensure we can safely access and update the key. */
const void *key = dictStoredKey2Key(d, ptr);
link = dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL);
serverAssert(link);

/* Setting key of existing dictEntry (newItem == 0)*/

if (*link == NULL) {
    *link = dictFindLink(d, addedKey, NULL);
    assert(*link != NULL);
}

findings.body:
`activeDefragHfieldAndUpdateRef()` now calls `dictStoredKey2Key()` to build a `key` it never uses. That helper is `static` in `dict.c` (line 97) and is not declared in `dict.h`, so `defrag.c` has no prototype and no definition to link against.

I compiled `defrag.c` with `-DHAVE_DEFRAG`. It emits `implicit declaration of function 'dictStoredKey2Key'` and `unused variable 'key'`, and `nm -u defrag.o` lists `dictStoredKey2Key` as undefined. `HAVE_DEFRAG` is defined whenever `USE_JEMALLOC` and `JEMALLOC_FRAG_HINT` are set (`src/zmalloc.h:79-81`), which is the default Linux build, so `redis-server` would fail to link. Under GCC 14+ the implicit declaration is a hard error.

The call is also dead: for hash dicts `keyFromStoredKey` is NULL, so the conversion is the identity. Passing `ptr` straight to `dictFindLink` is already correct because `Entry*` is the field sds (`src/entry.h:131-135`).

::diff{file="src/defrag.c" lines="277-286"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The build breaks only when `HAVE_DEFRAG` is defined, so a `make` without jemalloc or defrag support still passes. That makes this easy to miss in CI matrices that skip jemalloc.

Delete the unused `key` line and keep the existing `dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL)` call.

::suggestion{file="src/defrag.c" lines="279-282"}
    /* Before the key is released, obtain the link to
     * ensure we can safely access and update the key. */
    link = dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL);
    serverAssert(link);

In the `newItem == 0` branch, the relookup when `*link == NULL` calls `dictFindLink(d, addedKey, NULL)`. `addedKey` is the stored object itself when `keyDup` is NULL, for example a `kvobj*` in `dbDictType`. `dictFindLink()` expects a lookup key, so `dictSdsHash` would hash a `kvobj` header as if it were an sds.

The newItem=1 branch in the same function correctly calls `dictStoredKey2Key(d, key)` before lookup, so the two branches are now inconsistent. This is the exact class of bug the PR is meant to eliminate.

The path is latent. Every current caller (`db.c:574/583/790/2554`, `defrag.c:1020/1022/1412/1414`, `kvstore.c:882/886`) passes a link obtained from a prior lookup, so `*link` is never NULL here. A future caller that passes a NULL link would hash incorrectly, with no assertion to catch it, because the `assert(*link != NULL)` only fires after the wrong hash.

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="912-916"}

::callout{type="info"}
Using `addedKey` (rather than `key`) is correct when `keyDup` is set, because the duplicate has the same stored-object form. Only the conversion is missing.

::suggestion{file="src/dict.c" lines="912-916"}
    if (*link == NULL) {
        *link = dictFindLink(d, dictStoredKey2Key(d, addedKey), NULL);
        assert(*link != NULL);
    }

`validateStoredKeyConversion()` is defined but never called. A full `gcc -c -Wall -W dict.c` reports `'validateStoredKeyConversion' defined but not used`. Its logic is also muddled: it returns `extracted != NULL` for keyFromStoredKey dicts, which proves nothing about correctness, and `extracted == key` otherwise. It was apparently meant as a debug assertion but was never wired in.

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="97-108"}

If you want the check, make it a `debugAssert` at the call site in `dictAddRaw`. Otherwise delete it.

The old `dictUseStoredKeyApi(d, 1/0)` required every caller to set and reset a per-dict bit around each call. A missed reset would silently send later lookups down the stored-key hash and compare path. The PR removes the flag from `dict`, `dictType` and every call site: `t_hash.c`, `rdb.c`, `kvstore.c`, `module.c`, `defrag.c`. A repo-wide grep finds no remaining references to `dictUseStoredKeyApi`, `storedHashFunction`, `storedKeyCompare`, `useStoredKeyApi`, `dictHashKV`, `dictCompareKV` or the old 3-argument `hashTypeDelete`.

All eight `hashTypeDelete` call sites and the module.c caller are updated to the 2-argument form. For hash dicts, the removed `isSdsField` was already a no-op, because they have no storedHashFunction and their stored key is the field sds.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="1256-1294"}

`entryHashDictType` and `entryHashDictTypeWithHFE` do not set `keyFromStoredKey`. That is correct: `entryGetField()` casts the `Entry*` directly to the field sds (`src/entry.h:131-135`), so the stored object and the lookup key are the same bytes. `dictStoredKey2Key` then returns the pointer unchanged, and `dictSdsHash`/`dictSdsKeyCompare` work on `Entry*` without conversion.

This is a good reason for the callback to be optional. The db dicts are the only ones that need a conversion.

::code{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="69-80"}
